"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][21]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M21-L1 – Potential Outcomes and Causal Estimands<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define causal estimands and\ndistinguish identification from estimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two possible outcomes<\/h2>\n<p>A company wants to know whether a new maintenance programme reduces\nthe downtime of its machines. For machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_i=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote implementation and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_i=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe existing practice. The potential outcomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_i(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_i(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasure hours of downtime over the same specified period under the two\nalternatives. Under consistency,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_i=A_iY_i(1)+(1-A_i)Y_i(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe individual effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_i=Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a conventional parallel-group design, however, we observe only one of\nthe two outcomes. Even a perfect prediction of observed downtime cannot\nreconstruct the missing counterfactual without additional\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<p>The intervention must be precisely defined: which maintenance steps,\nwhich period, which machines? Different versions of the programme may\nhave different effects. Furthermore, maintenance on one machine may\nplace a greater load on a replacement machine. Such interference\nviolates the assumption that the outcome depends only on the unit’s own\nassignment. It may be necessary to use the workshop rather than the\nmachine as the experimental unit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimands and selection bias<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001668_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two potential outcomes per unit contrasted with the observed treatment assignment.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two potential outcomes per unit\ncontrasted with the observed treatment assignment.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Potential outcomes for failure hours.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The average treatment effect in the target population (ATE) is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[Y(1)-Y(0)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ATT refers to those actually treated:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[Y(1)-Y(0)\\mid A=1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA conditional average treatment effect (CATE) is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[Y(1)-Y(0)\\mid X=x]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative effect is desirable in our example. With heterogeneous\neffects, the figures depend on the target population.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Machine<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Observed<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table represents a fully known synthetic counterfactual world,\nnot an observable real-world dataset. ATE =\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3-4-1-2)/4=-2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours; ATT =\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. In contrast, the observed difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.5-5=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. Deliberately prioritising maintenance for problematic machines\nreverses the sign. In general,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[Y\\mid A=1]-\\mathbb E[Y\\mid A=0]=ATT+\\mathbb E[Y(0)\\mid A=1]-\\mathbb E[Y(0)\\mid A=0].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The last term is the selection difference in the untreated state (6\nhours here). The diagram shows both potential outcomes for each\nmachine.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y1<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">bool<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(a,y1,y0)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(y1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y0).mean()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> att<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(y1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y0)[a].mean()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> naive<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>y[a].mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y[<span class=\"op\">~<\/span>a].mean()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (ate,att,naive)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">3.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ate,att,naive)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Identification and study protocol<\/h2>\n<p>Under randomisation, assignment is independent of the potential\noutcomes (in the assignment distribution, not necessarily exactly in\nevery sample). Given consistency and positive assignment probability,\nthe difference in group means identifies the population ATE if the usual\nsampling assumption holds. With observational data, conditional\nexchangeability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⟂<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(a)\\perp A\\mid X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan play the same role, provided both treatments are possible for\nrelevant values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis assumption cannot be verified from observed data alone.\nIdentification asks whether the estimand follows from the observable\ndistribution; estimation asks how to obtain an estimate from finite\ndata; inference concerns its uncertainty.<\/p>\n<p>Before analysis, specify the target population, time of assignment,\nintervention, comparator, endpoint, observation period, handling of\nlosses and planned analysis. In the presence of non-compliance, the\neffect of assignment (intention-to-treat) may be a different estimand\nfrom the effect of treatment actually received.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Change the programme outcome of the fourth machine from 4 to 9 hours\nin the table. What happens? Solution: its effect increases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe ATE increases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ATT and observed difference remain unchanged because this machine is\nuntreated. This illustrates why observed data do not determine the ATE\nwithout further assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/03-potential_outcomes_and_randomization\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · potential outcomes,\nrandomisation and estimands<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M21-L2 – Causal Graphs and the Backdoor Criterion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Read DAGs, recognise confounding\npaths and derive suitable adjustment sets.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A graph is an assumption<\/h2>\n<p>A directed acyclic graph (DAG) contains nodes for variables and\narrows for assumed direct causal relationships relative to the chosen\nmodel. A machine with feedback over time can be modelled using separate\nstate nodes for each time index without creating a cycle within a DAG.\nAn absent arrow is also a substantive assumption. Data alone generally\ndo not determine the direction of every edge.<\/p>\n<p>Three elementary patterns are the chain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to M\\to Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe fork\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\leftarrow C\\to Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the collider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to K\\leftarrow Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA chain or fork is blocked by conditioning on the middle node; a\ncollider blocks the path without conditioning but opens it when\nconditioned on itself or a descendant. A path is blocked by a set if it\ncontains at least one conditioned non-collider or at least one collider\nfor which neither the collider nor any descendant is conditioned on. Two\nnodes are d-separated if all paths are blocked. Under the causal Markov\nassumption, conditional independences follow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adjustment or intervention?<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001540_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Directed graph distinguishes treatment, outcome and common causes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Directed graph distinguishes treatment,\noutcome and common causes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Maintenance causal graph and backdoor path.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The diagram depicts a fleet of machines: wear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninfluences the choice of maintenance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand downtime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMaintenance may also act through lubrication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA check measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis triggered by both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe backdoor set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{C\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains no descendant of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand blocks all paths from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat begin with an arrow pointing into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt thus preserves both the direct effect and the effect mediated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this graph,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare not suitable additional controls for the total effect.<\/p>\n<p>Under the assumed graph and identification conditions, for discrete\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[Y\\mid do(A=a)]=\\sum_c \\mathbb E[Y\\mid A=a,C=c]P(C=c).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The intervention replaces the assignment mechanism of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit is not the same as selecting observed treated units. The\ngroup-specific means are combined using the same target distribution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Wear<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Population proportion<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean without maintenance<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean with maintenance<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>low<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>high<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The standardised effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6(-1)+0.4(-4)=-2.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. Suppose 80% of treated machines and only 20% of untreated\nmachines have high wear; the crude difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7-5.6=1.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. With a treatment proportion of one third, these proportions\ncorrespond to the stated population.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y1<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>adjusted<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>(y1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y0)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y0<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([adjusted, raw],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.4<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(adjusted,raw)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Misspecification and hidden variables<\/h2>\n<p>An unmeasured factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsuch as latent load, could influence both quantities. Backdoor\nidentification must then be reconsidered. Even an extensive feature list\ndoes not guarantee valid adjustment: post-treatment variables may be\nmediators or selection nodes. Domain knowledge, timestamps, alternative\nplausible graphs and documented measurement processes are essential.\nCausal discovery can constrain graph structures under additional\nassumptions, such as the Markov property, faithfulness and, depending on\nthe algorithm, causal sufficiency; causally equivalent graphs can\nexhibit the same independences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A dataset contains only machines with a check measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCan the resulting bias automatically be eliminated by adjusting for\nwear? Solution: no. Selection conditions on the collider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to K\\leftarrow Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe non-causal path opened in this way is not blocked by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn additional assumption or a model of the selection process is\nrequired.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/04-directed_acyclical_graphs\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 4<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · DAGs and adjustment<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M21-L3 – Matching, Propensity Scores and Weighting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design and analyse observational\nstudies using balance, overlap and weighting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparable units<\/h2>\n<p>Matching seeks untreated counterparts for treated units with similar\npre-treatment characteristics. Exact matching requires selected\ncharacteristics to agree; distance matching uses a metric, such as\nEuclidean distance between standardised features. A maximum allowable\ndistance (caliper) can exclude poor matches. Matching without\nreplacement avoids reusing controls but can depend on the order.\nMatching with replacement can rely heavily on a few controls. Excluding\ntreated units with no comparable counterpart changes the target\npopulation; the resulting estimand is not automatically the original\nATT.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Propensity score and IPW<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001600_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Weighted comparison of exposure groups before and after covariate balancing.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Weighted comparison of exposure groups\nbefore and after covariate balancing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: IPW balance across workload groups.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The propensity score is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e(x)=P(A=1\\mid X=x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the correct score, the distribution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithin score levels is independent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder conditional exchangeability and positivity, this one-dimensional\nmeasure can be used for adjustment. For the ATE, the\nHorvitz–Thompson-type estimator is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>τ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\tau=\\frac1n\\sum_i\\left(\\frac{A_iY_i}{\\widehat e(X_i)}-\\frac{(1-A_i)Y_i}{1-\\widehat e(X_i)}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Conditional on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe expectation of the first term is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid A=1,X=x]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nsimilarly, the second yields the control mean. Alternatively, the Hájek\nestimator normalises each group by the sum of its weights; it is not\nidentical to the estimator above in a finite dataset. For the ATT,\ntreated units receive weight 1 and untreated units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e(X)/(1-e(X))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith appropriate normalisation. This again requires identifiability.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Load<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Number<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Treated<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Treated mean<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Untreated mean<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>low<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>high<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">13<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Before weighting, 80% of treated units have a high load, compared\nwith 20% of controls. Using the known stratum-specific scores of 0.2 and\n0.8 produces a weighted count of 100 per stratum in each group. The ATE\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the crude difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.8-9.6=2.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the change in the weighted distribution.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y1<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w1<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> w0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>e)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(w1<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>w0<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y0)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> naive<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y1<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>t.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y0<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>c.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w1,<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w0,ate,naive],[<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ate,naive)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Balance and overlap<\/h2>\n<p>Examine the score distributions separately by group, the standardised\nmean difference (difference in means divided by the pooled standard\ndeviation) for each important feature and, if necessary, higher moments.\nFor a binary feature such as high load, the unweighted difference here\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6/0.4=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is zero after weighting. A good treatment prediction model is not\nautomatically a good balancing model. Scores close to zero or one\nproduce extreme weights. The effective sample size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sum_i w_i)^2/\\sum_i w_i^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper group can fall sharply. Capping weights reduces variance but may\nintroduce bias; exclusion may change the population effect being\nestimated. Uncertainty must account for the sampling structure and the\nestimated scores; a simple unweighted t-test after matching is\ninappropriate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A treated machine has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is its ATE weight, and approximately how many control machines with\nthe same score correspond to this weight? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.04=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\none control has weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.96<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.042<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.96\\approx1.042<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso 24 such controls correspond to the weight. The single treated\nobservation has high leverage. When overlap is absent, greater model\ncomplexity cannot compensate for missing counterfactual information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/05-causal_cross_sectional_designs\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · matching and weighting<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M21-L4 – Instrumental Variables and Natural Experiments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess instruments and derive\nthe local effect of a treatment from variation in assignment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An indirect intervention<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001598_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A coupon affects participation, which may in turn affect the outcome.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A coupon affects participation, which may\nin turn affect the outcome.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Instrumental-variable assignment example.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A factory randomly distributes vouchers for an optional maintenance\nprogramme.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the voucher,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nactual participation and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndowntime. A valid instrument\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust have a measurable influence on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(relevance), be independent of potential outcomes and compliance types\n(or satisfy suitable conditional independence), and meet the exclusion\nrestriction:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naffects\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf voucher recipients also receive a diagnostic device that reduces\ndowntime independently of participation, the exclusion restriction is\nviolated. The diagram shows the permitted path and an unobserved common\ncause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof treatment and outcome.<\/p>\n<p>For a binary instrument and binary treatment, define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1), A(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompliers participate only with a voucher, always-takers in both cases,\nnever-takers in neither case, and defiers only without a voucher.\nMonotonicity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1)\\ge A(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrules out defiers. Under these assumptions, the Wald ratio identifies\nthe local average treatment effect for compliers (LATE):<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{LATE}=\\frac{\\mathbb E[Y\\mid Z=1]-\\mathbb E[Y\\mid Z=0]}{\\mathbb E[A\\mid Z=1]-\\mathbb E[A\\mid Z=0]}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The numerator difference is the intention-to-treat effect on the\noutcome, and the denominator difference is the proportion of compliers.\nUnder monotonicity, only these units change their behaviour, so the\nratio isolates their average effect. With heterogeneous effects, LATE is\ngenerally not the ATE.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical example and 2SLS<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Voucher<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Machines<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Participation rate<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean hours of downtime<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10.0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.70<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8.4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The first stage is 0.40; the reduced form is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. The ratio is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. In the simple linear case with one instrument and an intercept,\ntwo-stage least squares (2SLS) produces the same ratio. First,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis regressed on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(and exogenous controls if needed); then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis regressed on the predicted treatment value and those same controls.\nStandard errors require the correct IV covariance formula, not the naive\nsecond-stage OLS standard error.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.repeat([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.r_[np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>),np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">70<\/span>),np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">70<\/span>),np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">11.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a <span class=\"co\"># 10.0 und 8.4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>wald<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(y[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>].mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].mean())<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(a[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>].mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].mean())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.column_stack([np.ones(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(z)),z])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ahat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>X<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.linalg.lstsq(X,a,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>beta<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.lstsq(np.column_stack([np.ones(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(z)),ahat]),y,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([wald,beta,y[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].mean(),y[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>].mean()],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.4<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(wald,beta)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Natural experiments and weak instruments<\/h2>\n<p>An external allocation rule or an unexpected infrastructure change\nmay also affect participation. Such events are not random by definition.\nIt is necessary to examine whether other mechanisms simultaneously\naffect the outcome, whether groups were comparable before the event, and\nwhether individuals or firms could influence assignment. The exclusion\nrestriction typically cannot be established statistically alone. A small\ndenominator amplifies random error and even small violations of the\nassumptions; instrument strength must be assessed. With weak\ninstruments, inference procedures robust to weak identification\n(e.g. Anderson–Rubin tests in an appropriate linear model) are\npreferable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A second voucher changes the participation rate only from 0.30 to\n0.32, and mean downtime by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. The Wald estimate is also\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhy is this less convincing? Solution: the denominator is twenty times\nsmaller; an additional direct voucher effect of only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours would bias the ratio by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. Without credible assumptions and an uncertainty analysis, the\npoint estimate is not reliable evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/07-instrumental_variables\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 7<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · IV and LATE<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M21-L5 – Difference-in-Differences and Regression Discontinuity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify causal effects from\nchanges over time and threshold rules under explicit assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two groups and two time points<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001551_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two groups before and after treatment, showing the common-trend assumption.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two groups before and after treatment,\nshowing the common-trend assumption.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Difference-in-differences with parallel trends.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>One workshop introduces digital maintenance planning, while a\ncomparable workshop does not initially do so. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar Y_{g,t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote mean downtime in group\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand period\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference-in-differences (DiD) estimator is then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>τ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\tau=(\\bar Y_{1,post}-\\bar Y_{1,pre})-(\\bar Y_{0,post}-\\bar Y_{0,pre}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Before<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">After<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Change<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adoption<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">14<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparison<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The two groups start at different levels. Under parallel untreated\ntrends, the value for the adopting group would have changed from 14 to\n12 without the intervention. The effect is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9-12=-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. The diagram represents the assumed untreated trend with a dashed\nline. The assumption concerns the unobserved outcome without\nintervention, not equal starting values. Further requirements include no\nanticipation of treatment, no relevant spillovers and a suitable\ncomposition of the groups. Pre-intervention periods, placebos and event\nstudies help assess plausibility; a non-significant pre-trend does not\nprove parallel trends. With staggered adoption and changing effects, an\nordinary two-way fixed-effects model may contain misleading comparisons.\nGroup-time ATTs with suitable not-yet-treated or never-treated controls\nare an alternative. Standard errors should account for assignment and\ntemporal dependence (often by clustering at group level; special methods\nare needed when there are few groups).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Regression discontinuity (RD)<\/h2>\n<p>Machines now receive an inspection from 100 operating hours onwards.\nLet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the assignment variable minus the threshold, so that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\mathbf{1}(R\\ge0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a sharp RD design, the local estimand is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{RD}=\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\mathbb E[Y\\mid R=r]-\\lim_{r\\uparrow0}\\mathbb E[Y\\mid R=r].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Continuity of the conditional means of the untreated and treated\npotential outcomes at the threshold, absence of other discontinuous\nchanges and sufficient observations in its vicinity allow the difference\nto be interpreted as the effect at that threshold. Precise manipulation\nof the score can threaten this interpretation. In a fuzzy RD design,\nonly the treatment probability jumps; under IV-like assumptions, the\noutcome discontinuity is divided by the discontinuity in treatment\nprobability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synthetic local regression<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=-3,-2,-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=13+0.5R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non the right, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=11+0.2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1,2,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe lines fitted separately on the left and right have values of 13 and\n11 at the threshold. The local effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na global comparison of means would be a different measure because of the\nslopes. Real RD analyses often use local linear estimation with kernel\nweighting, bandwidth selection and bias-corrected confidence intervals.\nDensity tests, covariate continuity, alternative bandwidths and placebos\nare diagnostics, not proofs.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>did<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(r<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">13<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>r,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>r)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>left<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.polyfit(r[r<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],y[r<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> right<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.polyfit(r[r<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],y[r<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>right[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>left[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([did,rd],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(did,rd)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>During the implementation period, a new sensor reduces downtime by\none hour only in the treated workshop, independently of the programme.\nHow does this change the interpretation of DiD? Solution: under additive\neffects,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3=(-2)+(-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe estimate includes an extraneous effect. In RD, a second measure\nintroduced at the same threshold would be similarly problematic. These\ndesigns do not replace domain knowledge of the assignment process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/09-difference_in_differences\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 9<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · DiD<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mixtape.scunning.com/06-regression_discontinuity\">Cunningham:\nCausal Inference: The Remix, Chapter 6<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · RD<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M21-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M21-L6 – Mediation, Sensitivity Analysis and Causal Machine Learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Causal Inference and Empirical Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Decompose total effects, examine\nassumptions behind mechanisms and understand orthogonal estimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Direct and mediated effects<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001642_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic causal paths separating direct and mediated contributions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic causal paths separating direct\nand mediated contributions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Mediation and effect decomposition.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A training programme\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases the number of correctly performed inspections\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthese reduce downtime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(a,m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenoting the potential outcome under specified treatment and mediator\nvalues, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe potential mediator, the total effect is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TE=\\mathbb E[Y(1,M(1))-Y(0,M(0))].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>One possible decomposition adds and subtracts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(1,M(0))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe natural indirect effect is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[Y(1,M(1))-Y(1,M(0))]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the natural direct effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[Y(1,M(0))-Y(0,M(0))]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther reference levels yield different decompositions when there is\ninteraction. In a linear additive structural model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=2A+\\epsilon_M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>M<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>Y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=10-A-0.8M+\\epsilon_Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith suitably independent errors, the direct effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe indirect effect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(-0.8)=-1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the total effect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. This is illustrated in the diagram. Natural effects require\nstronger identification assumptions than a randomised total effect,\nparticularly regarding mediator–outcome confounding and possible\ntreatment-induced confounders. Randomising\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not randomise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA controlled direct effect, by contrast, sets the same mediator value in\nboth treatment situations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensitivity analysis<\/h2>\n<p>Many causal assumptions are only partly testable. Suppose an\nunobserved binary feature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases downtime additively by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours and is associated with treatment after adjustment for measured\nvariables. In a simple additive model within a covariate stratum, the\nresidual bias is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\delta[P(U=1\\mid A=1)-P(U=1\\mid A=0)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a prevalence difference of 0.25, it is +1 hour. Under this\nassumption, an apparent effect of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVary both quantities over ranges that are plausible in the domain. The\nresults are scenarios, not estimates of the unknown bias. For mediation,\na hypothetical correlation between structural errors can be examined;\nthe appropriate sensitivity parameterisation depends on the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Double machine learning (DML)<\/h2>\n<p>In a partially linear model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=\\theta A+g(X)+\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[\\epsilon\\mid A,X]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncomplex prior knowledge about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be incorporated using flexible models. Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(X)=\\mathbb E[A\\mid X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(X)=\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y-\\ell(X)=\\theta(A-m(X))+\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residuals permit<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>m<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>m<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\theta=\\frac{\\sum_i (A_i-\\widehat m_i)(Y_i-\\widehat\\ell_i)}{\\sum_i (A_i-\\widehat m_i)^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In cross-fitting, each observation is predicted exclusively by a\nnuisance model trained on other folds. The corresponding moment score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A-m(X))(Y-\\ell(X)-\\theta(A-m(X)))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis Neyman-orthogonal at the true model: small errors in nuisance\nquantities have no first-order effect on its expectation. This\nfacilitates inference given sufficient convergence rates, adequate\nvariation and appropriate sampling conditions. It remedies neither\nunmeasured confounding nor incorrect definitions of the estimand. With\nheterogeneous effects, the partially linear coefficient is generally not\nthe unweighted ATE. Causal forests and other CATE methods also need\nidentification assumptions and separate validation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> KFold<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> RandomForestRegressor<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rng.uniform(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,(n,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rng.binomial(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,p)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.empty(n)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ell<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.empty(n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> train,test <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> KFold(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,shuffle<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>,random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>).split(x):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> target,out <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(a,m),(y,ell)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        model<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>,min_samples_leaf<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>,n_jobs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        model.fit(x[train],target[train])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> out[test]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>model.predict(x[test])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ell<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> theta<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>u)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(theta<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(theta,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(y[a<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>].mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y[a<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].mean(),<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The synthetic example has the constant true effect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe crude difference is biased because assignment depends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe test checks reproducibility, not the validity of confidence\nintervals. With real data, the report should include robustness\nanalyses, a substantive justification for adjustment, uncertainty and\npossible spillovers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>With the mediator effect still\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe effect of training on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles from 2 to 4 in the linear example. The direct effect remains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution: indirect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntotal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. Can better predictive performance from a new ML model alone show\nthat this decomposition is causal? No, because it does not test the\nmissing counterfactuals and mediation assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://imai.fas.harvard.edu/research/mediation/\">Imai,\nKeele and Yamamoto (2010): Identification, Inference and Sensitivity\nAnalysis for Causal Mediation Effects<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · mediation identification and\nsensitivity<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~evans/APTS/double-machine-learning.html\">Didelez\nand Evans: Causal Inference, Chapter 20<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · cross-fitting and Neyman\northogonality<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://academic.oup.com/ectj/article/21/1/C1/5056401\">Chernozhukov\net al. (2018): Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and\nstructural parameters<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · search excerpt<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
